  "image_name": "4bcf9d3ec8a1a0bece0bdb5a4141db14.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how the gastrointestinal tract senses luminal contents and communicates with the brain, digestive glands, and immune system. The central image shows the stomach and intestines with highlighted sensing sites, linked by arrows to the brain, suggesting two-way gut–brain signaling involved in satiety and appetite.\n\nA key theme is that specialized epithelial cells detect material inside the gut. Enteroendocrine cells in the small and large intestine respond to bile acids, bacterial products, and metabolites, and release hormones such as CCK, ghrelin, GLP-1, GLP-2, GIP, 5HT, and PYY. A nearby G protein-coupled receptor icon indicates that receptor-mediated sensing underlies part of this process. In the small intestine, Paneth cells appear to respond to bacterial products by producing antimicrobial peptides, emphasizing host defense.\n\nThe right side highlights region-specific outputs. In the stomach, parietal and chief cells contribute to gastric secretion. In the small intestine and colon, tuft cells are linked to parasites and to IL-25 release with immune activation, while goblet cells drive mucus production. Overall, the figure presents the gut as a sensory and regulatory organ that integrates microbes, nutrients, and parasites to control digestion, immunity, and feeding behavior.",
